Justification: These classes separate responsibilities and make the program easier to maintain. For example, Vehicle stores common information, while Car and TwoWheeler can have different parking rates.
4. Important Business Rules
One parking slot can hold only one vehicle at a time.
Every parking ticket must have a unique ticket number.
A vehicle must be assigned only to an available slot.
An occupied slot must be released when the vehicle leaves.
Parking fees depend on vehicle type and parking duration.
Students and staff with valid monthly passes can park free of charge.
Visitors must pay the applicable parking fee.
Invalid floor and slot numbers must be rejected.
Duplicate active vehicle registrations must not be accepted.
Charging fees must be calculated separately for eligible electric vehicles.
5. Assumptions
Each vehicle has a unique registration number.
Parking slots are numbered starting from zero in the Java arrays.
Parking rates are fixed unless the pricing rules change.
Entry and exit times are recorded by the system.
A valid monthly pass is checked before calculating the fee.
Different floors may contain different numbers of slots.
6. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
Object-oriented approach
Each vehicle is represented by a Vehicle object containing its registration number, type, and related methods.
Searching for a vehicle in an array of n vehicles takes O(n) time in the worst case.
Parallel-array approach
The same information can be stored in separate arrays:
String[] regNo = new String[100];
String[] type = new String[100];
long[] entryTime = new long[100];
The information belonging to a vehicle must be kept at the same index in all arrays.
Searching for a registration number also takes O(n) time.
